A||||16||fiero56||helping a bored 3.4L(now 3.6) breathe better||||5||
Z||000000||fiero56||08-13-2001||10:34 PM||||I am trying to think of ways to help a local 3.6L Fiero owner help his engine breathe better(power drops markedly above 4K).<P>The best I can come up with, and I am wondering if I am missing anything is:<BR>"cold" air intake<BR>valve-job on the heads<BR>port stock exhaust manifolds<P>I figure the ported manifolds will yield the most noticeable improvement in upper rpm power, but couple that with re-worked heads and intake, and he could re-gain some lost power in the upper rpm range. What else should we look into? This is a brand new Grooms longblock with only a few thousand miles.||||reg||5
Z||000001||c m west||08-13-2001||10:41 PM||||how about a set of headers?||||reg||1
Z||000002||88formula||08-13-2001||11:15 PM||||Custom prom chip<BR>Port exhaust manifolds<BR>No cat<BR>Different intake and filter<BR>Performance exhaust<BR>Gasket and smooth intake runners<BR>Port heads and have a performance valve job done, fit them with SI valves<BR>Different more radical camshaft<BR>Ditch the fiero’s fuel injection system and go with a dual plane manifold and carburetor, it’s very restrictive<BR>Forced induction, nitrous, supercharger, turbocharger<P>Even my 3.4-liter engine runs out of breath with the modifications I have. My problem is the fiero TPI intake system is just too restrictive.  <BR><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||c m west||08-13-2001||11:35 PM||||word of advice, though many will tell you different, however from my expereince through MANY customers cars..... chips are an absolute waste of effort and hard earned cash||||reg||1
Z||000004||artherd||08-14-2001||06:38 AM||||Throttle body.  Has to be one of the most restrictive parts. get rid of it and go to something larger.<P>Also add cold-air while you're at it.<P>MSD ignition.<P>Cam.<P><BR>Lastly, turbo. Yeah.  It will help a lot (and be extreeeme :)<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000005||mrfixit58||08-14-2001||08:03 AM||||Your assesment of the power drop off is, I believe, right on. You've increased the engine displacement by 22%. You need to increase the throttle body by at least the same amount. Port match the intake to match the bigger throttle body. Port matching all the intake pieces will also help a little. <P>If you have an adjustable fuel pressure regulator, you can try bumping the pressure up a couple of pounds. I assume you're already running bigger injectors, right? <P>Look into a larger cross over pipe and a less restrictive muffler and cat. 2 1/2" pipe will help. I believe Chris West sells a special built Flowmaster for the Fiero. Others have had good luck with the muffler use on 3rd generation Camaros. <P>Have you ported the stock exhause mainfolds?Once you've experimented with all the above and you find you're still having power drop off problems, you may have to pop for some very expensive headers. But, more power = more $$$!<P>Keep us posted,<P>------------------<BR>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>87 GT    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/pennocksize.JPG">  <p>[This message has been edited by mrfixit58 (edited 08-14-2001).]||||reg||3
Z||000006||lou_dias||08-14-2001||08:35 AM||||the stock cam is limiting you so is the throttle body.  Bore the TB, port the intake and heads and use the factory 3.4 cam or crane HIPO, don't settle for the compucam unless you still want to turbo it.<P>biggest performance gains can be seen on the intake not the exhaust.  Engines are limited by how much fuel and air goes in not out.  Exhaust gas will be pushed out whether you have the factory setup or aftermarket.  Focus on the "in" side of things.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Carrolles||08-14-2001||09:07 AM||||I don't know if this is important to you or not but when I bought my long block from Grooms Ed Parks told me that if I did anything to the longblock (such as head work or cam) I'd void the warranty.||||reg||1
Z||000008||mrfixit58||08-14-2001||10:19 AM||||   <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by lou_dias:<BR><B>the stock cam is limiting you so is the throttle body.  Bore the TB, port the intake and heads and use the factory 3.4 cam or crane HIPO, </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Another alternative is to use 1.6 rocker arms instead of the 1.5. Talking to the guys in the speed shops, it's basically raise the peak HP by approximately 1000 RPMs.<P>  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by lou_dias:<BR><B> "biggest performance gains can be seen on the intake not the exhaust..."   "Exhaust gas will be pushed out whether you have the factory setup or aftermarket.  Focus on the "in" side of things."</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I'll have to disagree with this blanket statement. Have you ever swapped a 4bbl for a 2bbl on a motor with cast iron manifolds? I have and was very disappointed. After adding headers, I had to re-jet the carb because it was now too lean. Headers really woke up the motor.<P>I agree that the factory Fiero headers are pretty good if ported. That's why I didn't recommend exchanging them for the expensive full tube headers until he tried the things on the intake side first. However, the motor is now capable of pumping out at least 22% more gases (probably more with a bigger cam or 1.6 rockers) and I'm not sure the factory manifolds are up to the task.<P>  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by lou_dias:<BR><B> "Engines are limited by how much fuel and air goes in not out." </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Actually, that's totally untrue. Motors are limited only by HOW MUCH air flow through the motors. It's a total package, intake, cylinders, and exhaust. Ever had a catalytic converter plug up? The result is no power.<P>------------------<BR>Roy   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>87 GT     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/pennocksize.JPG">   <P>[This message has been edited by mrfixit58 (edited 08-14-2001).]<p>[This message has been edited by mrfixit58 (edited 08-14-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||2M4 Dale||08-14-2001||12:37 PM||||I agree with mrfixit58........you need total "flow" in and out.....back in the day the first thing we did to increase HP was to add a set of headers.....then the carb..cam..etc. Also your "friend" ... lol ... needs to bleed heat away from the engine......headers should help that too ! (get those nasty hot exhaust fumes outa there !)||||reg||1
Z||000010||fiero56||08-14-2001||08:58 PM||||Dale,<P>I heard the story <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">. You need to get that beast onto a dyno!||||reg||1
Z||000011||2M4 Dale||08-15-2001||01:03 AM||||Yeah !!!!! I think someone even offered to pay for it ... LMAO<P>This is just too much fun !!!!<P>Maybe I don't need a V6 'eh ???||||reg||1
Z||000012||lou_dias||08-15-2001||08:22 AM||||I'm not sure of all that's involved there, did you boost the fuel pressure there when you changed carbs?  That made you too rich maybe.  It's a fact that going from stock to headers on any car will make you run leaner because that unburned fuel leaves the combustion chamber or should I say "more of" the unburned fuel...  If you really had more air coming in and proper spark, you'd have less unburned fuel and more power.<P>What's the difference betweem a regular LS1 and the Z06 LS1... the intake primarily.  Infact the 2001 regular corvettes have more power this year because they use a common intake...the Z06 intake.  All changes to the Z06 over the standard LS1 are intake related (cam and computer dumping extra fuel since more air is coming in) and it's worth 40hp on the Z06.<P>Gee, what do turbos and superchargers do to giv you more power?  Do they<P>A) suck air out of your engine<BR>B) throw air into your engine<P>why is it that you can always throw more air+fuel in and always get more power yet when your exhaust is too big you lose power?<P>you tune your exhaust last to the amount of air your engine is putting out.<P>you don't go "gee I wnat to throw XXX amount of air out of my engine" and put on a 3" exhaust and expect more power.<P>a stock exhaust will flow more air out given more air in.  It can be improved, yes, but all you are going is optimizing flow, you are not enhancing it to perform better than it should be.||||reg||1
Z||000013||BENGAL4||08-15-2001||12:34 PM||||I think Dale's on the bottle.||||reg||10
Z||000014||lou_dias||08-15-2001||02:22 PM||||yeah my warranty is void but I had 25k miles worth of fun in 10 months.  threw another rod so I bought a stock 88GT while my buddy slowly rebuilds the 87.||||reg||1
Z||000015||mrfixit58||08-15-2001||06:51 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by lou_dias:<BR><B><P>What's the difference between a regular LS1 and the Z06 LS1... the intake primarily.  In fact the 2001 regular corvettes have more power this year because they use a common intake...the Z06 intake.  All changes to the Z06 over the standard LS1 are intake related (cam and computer dumping extra fuel since more air is coming in) and it's worth 40hp on the Z06.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I don't know the specifics but my guess would be, as you said in your last sentence: "optimizing flow". I would also guess that they've found a way to make the motor more efficient (ie., burning a higher % of the fuel that enters the motor and it will make more power). <P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by lou_dias:<BR><B><BR>Gee, what do turbos and superchargers do to give you more power?  Do they<P>A) suck air out of your engine<BR>B) throw air into your engine<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Apples and oranges. By super charging or turbo charging your increasing the volumetric efficiency of the motor (ie., if your cylinders normally hold one quart of air/fuel, a turbo or supercharger may fill it two or three as much or more. More air and fuel equals more power.)<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by lou_dias:<BR><B><BR>why is it that you can always throw more air+fuel in and always get more power yet when your exhaust is too big you lose power?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>You are correct to a point. One always needs to walk the fine line between too small and too big of exhaust. Pontiac did a pretty good job on the Fiero exhaust system. Other models, other makes are not a lucky.<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by lou_dias:<BR><B><BR>you tune your exhaust last to the amount of air your engine is putting out.<P>you don't go "gee I want to throw XXX amount of air out of my engine" and put on a 3" exhaust and expect more power.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Ditto my previous statement. But you can say: "Gee, I like the sound of a 3" exhaust so I'm going to build a motor to take advantage of it." But really, who does this. <P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by lou_dias:<BR><B><BR>a stock exhaust will flow more air out given more air in.  It can be improved, yes, but all you are going {doing?} is optimizing flow, you are not enhancing it to perform better than it should be.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Once again, you are correct to a point. The bigger the air pump, the bigger the exhaust needs to be to make PEAK power. IF stock manifolds were perfect and all you needed, NASCAR and the NHRA would be using them. Notice, one is normally aspirated, the other, naturally AND/OR super charged AND/OR turbo charged AND/OR NOS. <P>There are no flames intended. I think we agree on many points. And, if you'll re-read my previous point, you'll notice I, too, mention that buying aftermarket headers for his Fiero should come only AFTER he's explored the other options.<P>Peace,<P><P>------------------<BR>Roy :D<BR>87 GT   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/pennocksize.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000016||lou_dias||08-16-2001||02:14 AM||||No problem.<P>But my point is for individual mods and how much HP you can see.<P>A turbo is an airflow mod to the intake in my book, and so is a supercharger.  As it ram-air and an air filter and porting the TB and intake as well as headwork (which is usually both in and out).  Taking a stock motor and putting on the most extravagent exhaust on it will still only net you about 10 hp tops and you usually lose torque and fuel ecomony.  Where as the intake mods usually improve it and get you alot more hp.<P>Let me use an example:<P>add a turbo but keep the factory exhaust and you still gain 50 hp.  Then add a proper exhaust and gain another 25 hp.  75hp total...<P>now do it backwards...put on that exhaust first and  you get 8-10 hp, lose lots of torque and fuel ecomony.  Then add the turbo and we are on par again up to a 75hp gain.<P>if you could only do 1 thing at a time like most of us, which would be the more efficient way to go?  This is my point.<P>I agree with the grinding off of the exhaust manifolds if you have the time and tools but if you don't, you're better off boring the TB and intake and putting on a K&N and maybe a holley scoop and velocity tube (Chris West product).  And all together it's cheaper and you get more power out of it.<P>I know the thing to do is both...but if you wanna spend $550  on headers just to gain 8-10 hp, you could spend:<BR>$45 - velocity tube<BR>$35 - holley scoop<BR>$125 - TB and intake from Darrel Morse<BR>$30 - K&N air filter<P>it's alot cheaper and better for your car.<BR>Obviously the ram-air effect wouldn't show up on a dyno but it's still 2-4 hp for the k&N and 5 hp for the TB and intake on the dyno.||||reg||1
